<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Brahms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Brahms was a German composer and pianist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1862. Working within classical forms, he wrote four symphonies, concertos, chamber music, songs and A German Requiem, and was seen as the leading heir of Beethoven. He had close ties to Robert and Clara Schumann and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> court orchestra, which championed his works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms.jpg" alt="Johannes Brahms" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Brasch, Berlin (biography) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohannesBrahms_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
